.TH PDE 2
.SH NAME
pde: new, clear, get, set, splat, splatold, diffuse, wave, gray \- finite-difference field solvers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "pde.m";
pde := load Pde Pde->PATH;
Field: import pde;

f := pde->new(nx, ny, dx, dy, Pde->CLAMP);
pde->diffuse(f, diffusivity, dt);
pde->wave(f, speed, damping, dt);
pde->gray(a, b, da, db, feed, kill, dt);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pde
provides reusable explicit finite-difference solvers on rectangular scalar
fields.  A Field owns its current values, a work buffer, and the previous
time level required by the wave solver.  Values are stored row-major in
.BR u .
.PP
.B New
creates an nx by ny field with physical cell sizes dx and dy.  Boundary mode
.B CLAMP
imposes a zero normal derivative,
.B PERIODIC
wraps both axes, and
.B ZERO
uses zero outside the domain.
.B Clear,
.B Get,
.B Set,
.B Splat,
and
.B Splatold
initialize or interact with a field.
.PP
.B Diffuse
solves the two-dimensional heat equation.
.B Wave
solves a damped second-order wave equation; its Field's old array is the
previous time level.
.B Gray
advances two equally sized fields with the Gray-Scott reaction-diffusion
model.  All three routines automatically subdivide dt to satisfy a
conservative explicit stability limit.
.SS "The problem-specification language"
.EX
(p, err) := pde->newproblem(
	"mesh 64x64 domain 1x1 bc clamp\en" +
	"equation diffuse diffusivity 0.05\en" +
	"solver gmres tolerance 1e-8 restart 30\en" +
	"time step 0.01");
if(err != nil)
	...
err = pde->step(p, dt);
(nsteps, err) = pde->run(p, 10.0);
.EE
.B Newproblem
parses a spec into a ready
.B Problem
in one call, the same relationship a printf format string has to what
it produces.  A spec is one or more newline-separated lines:
.TP
.BI "mesh " NXxNY " [domain " WxH "] [bc clamp|periodic|zero]"
Delegates to
.IR mesh (2);
see there for the grammar.  Required.
.TP
.BI "equation diffuse diffusivity " D
.TP
.BI "equation wave speed " S " damping " Dm
Selects the equation and its parameters.  Required.  The two-field
Gray-Scott solver
.RI ( gray )
is not expressible here, since this spec describes one field; call
.B gray
directly as shown above.
.TP
.B "solver explicit"
The default: the same stability-limited
.B diffuse
/
.B wave
routines described above, internally subdividing dt as needed.
.TP
.BI "solver gmres|cg [tolerance " T "] [restart " R "] [maxiter " N ]
Solves
.B "equation diffuse"
implicitly (backward Euler) via
.IR krylov (2)
instead, matrix-free, unconditionally stable for any dt \(em unlike
.BR explicit ,
it never needs to subdivide the requested step.  The operator is
symmetric positive definite for
.B clamp
or
.B periodic
boundaries, so
.B cg
is valid (and faster) there;
.B gmres
is the safe general default.  Krylov internals never cross this
interface \(em nothing outside
.I pde.b
itself ever sees a
.IR krylov (2)
type.  Only valid with
.BR "equation diffuse" ;
.B "equation wave"
has no implicit solver in this tree yet and rejects any solver but
.BR explicit .
.TP
.BI "time step " DT
Sets the default step
.B run
uses; optional, but required before calling
.BR run .
.PP
.B Step
advances
.I p
by an explicit
.I dt
(an interactive caller, e.g. a redraw loop, picks its own
.I dt
each call).
.B Run
is the batch/unattended driver: it repeats
.B step
using
.IR p 's
own
.B "time step"
until at least
.I until
total time has elapsed, returning the step count \(em what a
verification or UQ study, run without a redraw loop, calls instead of
.BR step .
Both return an error string (e.g. a solver that failed to converge)
rather than silently leaving
.I p.field
holding a garbage state.
